## What to check

**Does every `<process_inventory>` entry have a real rationale?** A
`category` with a `<rationale>` that just restates the category name
("sharded because it's sharded") is the exact improvisation-at-impl-time
failure this element exists to prevent (v5 §2.5). Demand the actual
argument: what state does this process hold, what happens if a node
holding it disappears, why does that force this category and not a
plainer one. `local` needs justifying too — "doesn't need to survive a
node loss" is an argument; silence is not.

**Is `<privapi>` actually private?** Compare it against
`{{ parent }}`'s `<pubapi>`. If an entry in this draft's `<privapi>`
is the same surface, or trivially the same surface renamed, the
subcomponent has leaked the component's public boundary down a level
instead of owning a genuinely internal one — flag the specific entry
and say which pubapi function it duplicates.

**If this is the store subcomponent, are the queries function-shaped?**
Read `<store><schemas>` for anything that reads like generic repository
CRUD (`get/1`, `list/1`, `update/2` with no caller-specific meaning) —
that's exactly what v5 §2.4 rules out. Every query should be named for
what the caller actually needs. If `<grade>` is present, check it's
warranted — only the event-sourced store family (bare_log or
commanded_aggregate) carries it; a plain Ecto store declaring one is
either wrong or under-explained.

**Overlap with siblings.** Cross-reference this draft's `<privapi>`
against `{{ siblings }}` — two subcomponents each exposing a
same-shaped surface for the same concept is a seam nobody decided,
just accreted; name both and ask which one should own it.

**Does `<techspec>` earn the split from siblings?** The draft should
argue why this slice belongs to this subcomponent and not a sibling.
If the boundary reads arbitrary, that's a finding worth raising even
if nothing downstream is broken yet — an arbitrary internal seam is
exactly the drift the private/public split is supposed to prevent.

Return a `<review verdict="approve|revise">` with a `<summary>` and one
`<finding severity="blocking|advisory">` per issue — each `<location>`
naming the process name, privapi entry, or store query at fault, each
`<comment>` saying what a passing version would contain.
